1️⃣ Core Idea

Instead of uploading and storing files as a whole, the system splits files into smaller chunks and uploads only the changed chunks. Metadata is used to reconstruct files and synchronize updates across devices.

This approach:

Reduces bandwidth usage

Saves cloud storage

Improves upload speed

Supports file versioning and conflict resolution

2️⃣ Chunking & Metadata

Files are broken into content-based chunks, and each chunk is identified using a hash of its content.

If a chunk doesn’t change, it is not uploaded again

Identical chunks are stored only once (deduplication)

Metadata stores:

File information (name, size, version)

Ordered list of chunk hashes

Ownership and workspace details

Metadata acts as instructions to reassemble the file during download or sync.

3️⃣ Desktop Client Architecture

The desktop client is responsible for detecting file changes and synchronizing them with the cloud.

It has four main components:

🔹 Watcher

Continuously monitors sync folders and detects file create, update, or delete events.

🔹 Chunker

Splits files into chunks and identifies which chunks have changed. Only the modified chunks are uploaded.

This improves:

Network efficiency

Upload speed

Parallelism

🔹 Indexer

Acts as the coordinator. It processes events from the Watcher, manages file versions, updates the internal database, and communicates metadata updates to the backend using a message queue.

🔹 Internal Database

Stores local state such as chunk mappings, file versions, upload status, and offline changes.

It enables:

Offline support

Crash recovery

Faster operations

Conflict detection

The internal database is the client’s local source of truth.

4️⃣ Metadata Database vs Internal Database

The Metadata Database is the global source of truth stored in the cloud, while the Internal Database is the client’s operational memory.

Metadata DB supports synchronization across users and devices

Internal DB supports offline mode, retries, and performance optimization

Both are required.

5️⃣ Message Queuing Service

A message queue enables asynchronous and loosely coupled communication between clients and the Synchronization Service.

There are two types of queues:

🔹 Request Queue (Global)

All clients send metadata update requests here

Multiple Synchronization Service instances consume requests

Provides load balancing and scalability

🔹 Response Queues (Per Client)

Each client has its own queue

Used to deliver update notifications

Required because messages are deleted once consumed

6️⃣ Synchronization Service

The Synchronization Service is the core component responsible for maintaining consistency across clients.

It:

Processes file update requests

Validates changes against metadata

Updates the Metadata Database

Notifies all subscribed clients

Subscribed clients are:

Devices or users that have access to a file, folder, or workspace and need to receive updates.

Offline clients:

Poll the service when they reconnect

Receive only missed updates

7️⃣ Cloud Storage

Cloud Storage stores the actual file chunks.

Clients directly upload and download chunks using cloud APIs

No need to build custom storage

Services like Amazon S3 can be used

Google Drive uses Google’s own internal storage systems, but in real designs we typically use managed storage like S3.

8️⃣ End-to-End Flow (Quick)

User modifies a file

Watcher detects the change

Chunker splits the file

Changed chunks are uploaded to cloud storage

Indexer updates local DB

Synchronization Service updates metadata

Subscribed clients are notified and synced

🎯 Final One-Line Summary
This architecture uses chunk-based uploads, metadata-driven synchronization, asynchronous messaging, and cloud storage to efficiently support file syncing, versioning, offline access, and scalability across multiple devices.


